{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"Woe to you, O Moab! You are lost, O people of Chemosh\"</b> — for the people of Moab would worship Chemosh their god, building high places and trusting in him more than any people, and in the time of their distress they would say to him: \"Arise and save us.\" And thus Scripture mentions this concerning them everywhere, as it is written: \"And when it appears that Moab is weary on the high place, he comes to his sanctuary to pray but cannot\" (Isaiah 16:12), and it is stated: \"And Chemosh goes into exile, his priests and his princes together\" (Jeremiah 48:7), \"And I will bring an end to Moab, says YHWH — him who offers on the high place and burns incense to his god\" (ibid. verse 35). Therefore the speakers of the parable say by way of mockery that Chemosh gave his sons as fugitives — that is, fleeing and running away from the sword — and his daughters into captivity, for those who believe in him are called his sons and daughters just as his people are called. And similarly, \"the daughter of a strange god\" (Malachi 2:11). And not according to Rashi's explanation, who explained: he gave — the one who gives — his sons as fugitives from the sword. Now Chemosh was also worshipped by the children of Ammon, as Jephthah said: \"whatever Chemosh your god gives you to possess, you shall possess\" (Judges 11:24), and they had additionally in their land \"Milcom, the abomination of the Ammonites\" (I Kings 11:5). Or perhaps Jephthah mentioned Chemosh to Ammon on account of what Israel took from Moab, just as he mentioned to them (ibid. verse 25) Balak king of Moab — to say that neither Chemosh their god nor Balak their king saved their land from the hand of Israel. And it is very fitting to say according to the words of our Sages (Bemidbar Rabbah 19:18) that the speakers of the parable are Balaam and those like him among the soothsayers who would utter parables about the future:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Rashi cited here explains נתן as referring to an Israelite who would give his sons as fugitives; Ramban rejects this and reads the subject as Chemosh himself."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:36.277395101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}